title The part that grew
prompt raise the card bound.
think Derived rather than picked, because the elder raise set that precedent in its own words -- the number is derived rather than granted -- and a bound chosen by feel is the thing this law exists to refuse.
think Measured the card's three parts the same way the elder raise measured them, so the two numbers are comparable rather than merely both true.
think Found that only ONE of the three parts moved. The standing block reads 7,008 today and read 7,008 on 20260904, byte for byte, still fourteen directives. The durable spine drifted 521 bytes. The live front went 5,161 to 12,814.
think Divided the live front by the fleet each time and the unit held: 1,720 per ship at three ships, 1,602 per ship at eight. Two independent measurements five ships apart, so the live front is not swelling -- it is being multiplied. That is what makes the raise derivable.
think Allocated it the way the standing block was already allocated: count what it holds, round to a power of two above the measurement. Eight ships at 2,048 each is 16,384, where 2,048 leaves a ship room to say more than 1,602 without forcing a rewrite.
obs 8,192 + 16,384 + 16,384 = 40,960. Deliberately NOT a power of two: 32,768 was one only because its three parts happened to sum to it, and inflating to 65,536 to keep the shape would be granting a number rather than deriving one.
obs WHAT FORCED IT, COUNTED. construction/CHECKPOINTS.md records six sweeps whose stated reason is the card standing at or over 32,768 -- 32,744, 32,765, 32,838, 33,165, 34,086 -- and eight checkpoints were written on 20260905 alone, by more than one ship, several within the same hour. A bound that forces one condensation is working; one that forces six is sized for a smaller fleet.
obs The number is stated once. tools/fixtures/l/living_pin_max_bytes.sh writes no copy of it and reads the seated law, so raising it meant editing one line of one spec and the script answered 40960 immediately. The card's own header states it a second time on purpose, for a reader rather than a tool, and that copy was updated in the same breath.
obs The general bound stays 24,576 and the session-logs index keeps its own 57,344. One exception moved; nothing else did.
obs THE HONEST LIMIT, and it is not solved here. The live front scales with the fleet, so twelve ships re-open this arithmetic at 49,152. Raising the ceiling each time the fleet grows is a treadmill, and the exit is not a bigger number -- it is a live front that does not hold one region per ship. That is a design question rather than a bound, and it is named in the law so the next raise meets the question rather than more arithmetic.
obs Cost named rather than hidden: the card is read WHOLE every lap by every body, so this is ~10k tokens per lap per ship rather than ~8k, about 16k more per lap across eight. The measured alternative was this evening -- four condensations of one entry by one hand, plus a peer's fold of two closed accounts an hour earlier, all to fit one round.
obs A PEER'S NEW GUARD CAUGHT ME THE LAP IT ARRIVED. log_has_a_row landed on the roster while this round ran -- 116 guards where the morning had 114 -- and it read pin_shelves_missing=1: I had opened a 20260906 shelf and left the living pin still naming 20260905 as the open day. The shelf, the room, and the pin are meant to describe one set, and mine described two.
obs Worth recording because of WHEN it fired. A guard written by another ship, rostered hours ago, caught the first hand to open a new day after it landed. That is the fleet working the way it was drawn -- a peer's instrument reading my work before Keaton or I did.
obs Three roster pages needed the same fact and each states it separately: CHAPTERS.md (the roster of shelves), README.md (the living pin's day table), and the shelf itself. I wrote two and missed the third, which is exactly the shape a guard exists for.
obs THE COUNT DRIFTED WHILE I WROTE IT. I closed 20260905 at 53 rows, measured; by the time the round landed the shelf held 57, four peer logs stamped as late as 23:47:01 having arrived after my count. The guard read pin_count_drift=1 and named it. Both roster pages carry 57 now.
obs That is a different fault from the missing row, and worth separating: the first was a page I forgot to write, the second a number that was true when written and false forty minutes later. On an eight-ship pier a count is a measurement with a shelf life, and the only safe place to take it is the moment before the commit.
obs FIVE REBASES TO LAND ONE COMMIT, and the reason is arithmetic rather than bad luck. A full roster pass on this pier now runs 20 to 40 minutes under eight-ship contention, and peers land every ten or so, so a pass started after a rebase cannot finish before the next one is owed. The card names the escape -- --scoped reproves only what moved since a FULL GREEN receipt -- and I cannot use it, because agent_jail reds every pass and a red guard withholds the receipt. That is exactly the compounding cost %431 priced and %444 removed for the seed publisher, arriving on my own lap in a different material.
obs So this round's final verification is NAMED rather than implied: five witnesses my change can actually affect were run GREEN on the rebased tree -- log_has_a_row, declared_ceiling, remember_git_nib, tracked_link, index_row_bound -- and the last full 124-guard pass was GREEN at 119 with tree_moved=no on the tree one rebase earlier. A full pass on the final tree was not run, because starting one guarantees another rebase. Saying which half is proven costs a sentence; implying both would cost the reader.
obs Three merge faults in one rebase, each a different shape. A count I had measured (57) against a peer's fresher one (58) -- take the measurement, never either memory. A row of mine that landed BELOW an older row when two shelves merged, which index_row_bound caught as rows_misordered. And a correction of mine that reverted when I took upstream's side wholesale: 20260904 went back to open after I had closed it at 45. Resolving a conflict by choosing a side discards your own true edits along with your stale ones.
loom card_bytes=32926 bound_before=32768 bound_after=40960 standing=7008 live_front=12814 spine=12927 ships=8 per_ship_live=1602 headroom=8034
